sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hamiltons-rule-exact-scope--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Computational phase map of when parameter-based Hamilton's rule predicts selection" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hamiltons-rule-exact-scope> ;
    dct:description "Map the boundary between readings (i) and (iii) across model space, not just at isolated counterexamples. Model classes: Wright–Fisher island model (n demes ∈ {2, 5, 20}, migration m ∈ [0.001, 0.5]); Moran process on cycles, regular graphs (degree 3–10) and random graphs with N ∈ {20, 50, 200}; two-player games with synergy d ∈ [−b, b]; and multiplicative versus additive fitness, at selection strengths δ from 10^-4 to 1. In each cell compute exactly (small N, linear algebra on the Markov chain) or by simulation with 10^6 replicates (larger N) the fixation probability of a single cooperator relative to neutral 1/N. Compare three predictors: parameter HR with model b, c and pedigree or identity-by-descent r; the weak-selection inclusive-fitness condition (Taylor–Frank); and regression HR, which holds by approach 1. Output the fraction of model space where parameter HR gives the right sign, as a function of δ and d." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Phase maps showing, for each model class, the region where parameter-based r b > c correctly predicts the direction of selection. The pre-stated expectation is 100% agreement at d = 0, δ → 0, with measured degradation as |d| and δ grow; a region of disagreement at d = 0 under weak selection would itself be notable." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Monte Carlo noise at small fixation-probability differences makes signs undecidable in large parts of the grid (Wilson intervals overlapping 1/N), so the map is mostly 'unresolved'." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/computation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement exact Markov-chain fixation for small graphs and validated Monte Carlo for larger ones. 2. Pre-register the predictors and the agreement metric (sign agreement and, where nonzero, rank correlation of effect sizes). 3. Sweep the grid; publish the phase maps with code and seeds. 4. Identify the analytic boundary (for example agreement whenever d = 0 and δ → 0) and feed it back to approach 1 as a theorem to prove." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: large-scale-computation" ;
    skos:note "Tool: markov-chains" ;
    skos:note "Tool: population-genetics" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hamilton-rule> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hamilton-haplodiploidy> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hamilton-lmc> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1420-9101.2006.01119.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09205> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hamiltons-rule-exact-scope> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "The exact scope of Hamilton's rule" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1420-9101.2006.01119.x> dct:title "The evolution of cooperation and altruism – a general framework and a classification of models" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Lehmann and Keller 2006, model classification used to define the grid" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09205> dct:title "The evolution of eusociality" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Nowak, Tarnita and Wilson 2010, the models they claim fall outside inclusive fitness" .
}

